From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 05:03:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 286: the ablation pair localises the inversion in the
 reading, not the mix

The nohid arm had run two hours BEFORE the hidden arm on the same
second-generation code - the judge numbers are byte-identical between the two
JSONs, so the tapes and examples were identical too. That upgrades the pair
from two runs to a controlled ablation, and it falsifies the prediction the
previous commit staked: the composition flip lives in the count features, so
it had to show in the features-only arm. It did not show.

  same code, same tapes        nohid                   hidden
  LOO accuracy @ coverage      0.641 @ 0.557           0.325 @ 0.571
  selective curve              1.00 -> 0.43 falling    0.29 -> 0.44 rising
  unknown_auc                  0.412 (1 sigma, noise)  0.259 (2.8 sigma, inverted)
  matched vs votes / return    tie 0.431 / 1.000 (n=9) loses both
  lying tape at votes' cov     0.487 vs 0.462          loses
  verdict                      EVIDENCE_PARTIAL        EVIDENCE_NO

So the sampler mismatch is real but survivable - nine numbers trained on the
wrong mix still rank correctly on the right one, and nohid passes
G_learns_evidence and G_survives_lie, the first non-vacuous pass of either.
What is not survivable is the reading: on identical tapes it halved accuracy
at equal coverage and inverted the confidence ordering. The features-only
ablation beats the arm carrying the stage's whole claim.

Two hypotheses fit "confidently wrong held-out, only when reading":
  a) the trunk fine-tunes at lr_upper over 6000 steps on resamples of the
     same 25k lines and memorises subject->value; held-out subjects then
     misfire with confidence. Facts moving into weights - the exact leak the
     separation invariant forbids, showing up as its own predicted signature.
  b) the reading channel (~512 dims against 9) drowns the features and the
     head overfits reading noise. Generic overfit, no memorisation needed.

--frozen-trunk separates them: reading stays, trunk learning stops, so (a)
predicts frozen-hidden recovers to nohid and (b) predicts it stays broken.
Added head_input_norms to the decision JSON - the head's first-layer norm
split per input dimension between reading and features - which bounds how
much room (b) has before that run is spent.

Run order that spends the least: nohid on the new code first (~12 min; if
the sampler fix lifts the AUC past 0.5 the ablation arm passes EVIDENCE_OK
outright), then hidden --frozen-trunk (~2.4 h, the discriminator), then
unfrozen hidden only if frozen recovers. And if nohid passes fully while
every hidden arm fails, that is the stage's finding rather than a bug: at
this scale judgment lives in the counters, and "the weights compare
readings" is unsupported. HANDOFF.md section 6 says so in those words.

